ad-switch PMOS is normally about 10K, so that the device turns off reliably. A 210K pull-up would weaken the gate drive and slow switching.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"schematic\", \"quote\": \"Q9 AO3401A 1 3 R48 210K\"},\n        {\"source\": \"schematic\", \"quote\": \"+3V3 1 3 2 Q9 AO3401A +3V3 10K R48\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If the 210K value is real, the controlled rail may turn off or on slowly, or may not be pulled firmly high when the enable is released.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.15\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The text-extracted drawing suggests the U12 buck feedback divider (R17 1.8M / R16 205k) does not set a 5 V output, which would put the +5V rail far above its intended level. The R48 value also reads inconsistently between two copies of the drawing. Both findings are based on the drawing text and are not confirmed against the image.\"\n}",
